@page "/components/card"
@model HTMX.Application.Web.Pages.Components.Card.IndexModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Card";
    ViewData["Section"] = "Components";
}
@*
    The Card component is this folder: CardTagHelper.cs holds the utilities of the card and each of its parts, and a
    view names them in the card attribute on the elements it writes, so changing them there changes every card in the
    application. Every section of this page is a card, and each says which of the card's parts it shows, as the brand
    gives them (brand-htmlx-pages, Card).
*@
<header page-header>
    <nav breadcrumbs aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <ol><li><a asp-page="/Index">Home</a></li><li><a asp-page="/Components/Index">Components</a></li><li><span aria-current="page">Card</span></li></ol>
    </nav>
    <div page-header="title-row">
        <h1 tabindex="-1" autofocus="@Model.HeadingFocused">Card</h1>
    </div>
    <p class="text-fg-muted">
        The surface every list, form and panel in the application sits on, drawn by one tag helper, which sits beside
        this page. Every section of this page is a card.
    </p>
</header>

<section card aria-labelledby="parts-heading">
    <header card="header"><h2 id="parts-heading">Parts</h2></header>
    <div grid="scroll">
        <table grid>
            <caption class="sr-only">The card's parts, with the attribute that names each and what each holds</caption>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Part</th>
                    <th scope="col">Attribute</th>
                    <th scope="col">What it is</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td class="whitespace-nowrap">The card</td>
                    <td class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>card</code></td>
                    <td>
                        The surface, with a border, rounded corners and a shadow. It is a size container, so a component
                        inside it adapts to the card's width, at 560px, rather than to the window's.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="whitespace-nowrap">Header</td>
                    <td class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>card="header"</code></td>
                    <td>
                        Optional: the card's title, an h2, and at most one action at its right, with a line below.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="whitespace-nowrap">Body</td>
                    <td class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>card="body"</code></td>
                    <td>
                        16px of padding around what the card holds. A table or a list that runs to the card's edges,
                        like this one, sits in the card without a body.
                    </td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</section>

<section card aria-labelledby="action-heading">
    <header card="header">
        <h2 id="action-heading">A header with an action</h2>
        <a button="secondary" asp-page="/Components/Index">All components</a>
    </header>
    <div card="body">
        <p>
            This card's header holds its title and one action, a link back to the Components list, at its right. Its
            body holds this paragraph.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<div card>
    <div card="body">
        <p>
            This card has no header, only a body: a card that needs no title, such as a not-found message, is a
            <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> rather than a <code>&lt;section&gt;</code>, since it is not a part of the page a
            reader would look for by name.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<section card aria-labelledby="accessibility-heading">
    <header card="header"><h2 id="accessibility-heading">Accessibility</h2></header>
    <div card="body">
        <ul class="m-0 grid list-disc gap-2 pl-5">
            <li>
                A card with a title is a <code>&lt;section&gt;</code> named by that title through
                <code>aria-labelledby</code>, so a screen reader can list it and go straight to it.
            </li>
            <li>Its title is an h2, so the page's cards make its outline under the page's h1.</li>
            <li>In forced colors its shadow goes and its border stays, so the card keeps its edge.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</section>
